{
  "$comment": "Opt-in cloud guardrails, merged by hand into ~/.claude/settings.json (README 'Cloud guardrails'; the installer does not apply this file). Only ask rules, so it widens nothing: an ask rule wins over an allow rule and over a skill's allowed-tools, prompts in every permission mode (auto and bypassPermissions too; dontAsk and claude -p refuse instead) and matches past a leading VAR=value, timeout/time/nice/nohup/stdbuf/command/builtin and a bare xargs. No deny rules: the engineer runs every one of these commands on purpose, and a deny would also block them when asked for explicitly. Every rule is spelled twice: `tool VERB *` (it also matches the bare verb) and `tool -* VERB *` for flags before the verb (terraform -chdir=x apply, kubectl --context prod -n x delete, helm --kube-context x upgrade); verbs that run without arguments also have `tool -* VERB`. In a verb of two words the first word carries a wildcard (`helm get* values *`, `kubectl rollout* restart *`, `kubectl config* use-context *`), so flags may also stand between the words (helm get -n x values r); Terraform takes no flags there. az has no flags-before form: its rules are `az * VERB *`, any text before the verb. Each rule is mirrored for the PowerShell tool (case-insensitive there). Groups: terraform apply/destroy/refresh/import/taint/untaint/force-unlock, state rm|mv|push and workspace new|select|delete change state or infrastructure; terraform state pull, output -json|-raw and show -json print state values. kubectl apply/create/delete/replace/edit/patch/scale/autoscale/set/label/annotate/taint/cordon/uncordon/drain/expose/run and rollout restart|undo|pause|resume change the cluster; exec/cp/debug/port-forward enter it; config use-context/set*/unset/delete-*/rename-context change the kubeconfig (use-context redirects every later kubectl, also in other terminals); kubectl get *secret* (also a name that contains secret; lower case in Bash) and config view --raw|--flatten print secret values. helm install/upgrade/uninstall/delete/rollback/push and plugin install|update change releases or registries or run plugin code; helm get values|manifest|hooks|all print values and rendered Secrets. az create/delete/update/set/add/remove/reset/start/stop/restart/deallocate/scale/upgrade/purge/recover/restore/import/move/assign/invoke/run/deploy, pipelines build queue, create-for-rbac, get-credentials, update-credentials, rotate-certs, enable-addons, disable-addons, delete-machines, set-policy, delete-policy, az login, az rest (any method) and az upgrade (the CLI itself) change Azure, Azure DevOps or the CLI identity; az keyvault secret show|download, account get-access-token, list-keys, keys list, credential show, appsettings list and acr login --expose-token print secrets or tokens. They override these permissions.json allow rules and nothing else: kubectl get * (secret forms), helm get * (values, manifest, hooks, all), terraform output * (-json, -raw) and terraform show * (-json). A backstop, not a sandbox: a quoted or escaped verb (kubectl 'delete'), bash -c, sh -c, eval, xargs with flags, a verb from the environment or a default value (terraform $VERB), an alias or function, a path-qualified tool (./terraform apply, terraform.exe apply), a wrapper that Claude Code does not strip (sudo, env, watch) and an upper-case Bash spelling (kubectl get Secret) are not matched. Nor is a verb that is not listed: the lists are a sample (terraform test, terraform init -migrate-state, helm test, kubectl attach|proxy and az storage account show-connection-string|generate-sas are not asked about), and terraform output <name> can print a sensitive value that no rule asks about. --dry-run does not exempt a command. Rules match words, not meaning, so there are false positives: with flags before the verb any later word that is a verb counts (kubectl --context prod auth can-i create pods, kubectl -n x logs deploy/web -c debug, helm -n x diff upgrade r c), kubectl get asks for a name that contains secret, az * set * for a resource called set, and Claude Code also matches each rule against the whole command line, so text after a pipe or && can trigger one (kubectl get pods | grep secret, az account show && npm run build).",
